The simulator starts each step by asking the user for the values of the input variables. It only accepts an input that is the right size and made up only of 1s and 0s. Then all equations are processed in an order worked out by the scheduler. To store the values associated with each identifier, we use a hashtable. A new hashtable is created each round, the one from the previous round is kept for reg instructions. For the first round, all reg instructions will return only bits set to false. At the end of each round, the simulator prints the values of the output identifiers. 

A "memory space" is mapped to each instruction using a hashtable. A memory space is an array of size word_size*2^addr_size. Each memory address is thought to point to memory word, therefore there is no overlap between memory addresses. Writing to the ram is only done after all equations have been processed. The memory spaces used for rom instructions have all their bits set to False. 

There is not static testing of the consistency of the types. However at runtime the program does test that variables are assigned values that are the of the right type. As such the following program raises an InvalidType exception : 
...
VAR a:4, b
IN
b = a

the file netlist_simulator.ml itself starts with utilitaries that are used throughout the file. Then, come the functions responsible for input/output with the user. The function eval_expr evaluates expressions to a certain value. Then there are two functions responsible for ram allocation and updating. Finally, the function simulator is in charge of running each round of simulation using all the previous functions. 

In my opinion, the main difficulty stemmed from ambiguous instructions. The program scheduler_test.byte seems to work no matter the order of the equations that scheduler.schedule returns. The ram and rom instructions were not immediately clear as each equation having its own ram runs counter to how we have always been taught to think of and use ram. The fact that scheduler_test.byte does not implement these instruction and therefore cannot be used to check what the expected behavior is was all the more frustrating. There are also implicit type conversions that are not indicated anywhere. The test ram.net, line 19 has the equation "_l_19 = SLICE 1 1 _l_14" where SLICE returns a bit rather than a bitarray of size 1 as the documentation would suggest. Similary, in nadder.net, line 19 has the expression "CONCAT res_rest res_i" which concatenates res_i, which is a bit and not a bitarray. 
